#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537" :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$, shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/611bd674f62d8f3f4a37069e92e426cd.png)\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$.\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C$, it neither helps nor hinders its motion.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/84d5f3fc4f5cfdf0b01cdab07f394220.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C$, then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/6155f43399f2bd6f77151753d036ffa3.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = \\sin t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nF(r(t)) =-yi + xj =-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&= \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ = ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}dt \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179535 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =(e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$, then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$, that is\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0\n$$\n\nWe notice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = e^y\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822d3bef-65b5-5a99-ac7e-e20a62916224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177725} {:answer/id #uuid "90d6d082-eff8-5dfc-8f21-983e899f0608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177726} {:answer/id #uuid "09c2c3aa-e3f6-5605-a394-410e31dd85d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177727} {:answer/id #uuid "147f4c5a-601c-5304-85da-96bf081c2373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177728} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0b1335-20bc-54f9-86d2-293f1de83b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f0b1335-20bc-54f9-86d2-293f1de83b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177729} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f48a77-5249-564f-adca-ba17a4720b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177724}] :question/id #uuid "889fbef5-9dc3-5991-a476-ffe3c0711611" :question/math-academy-id "q-125395" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3713/Source/Images/q-125395.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F$, shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $∮_{C}F \\cdot dr = 0$\n3. $∮_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066b1031-1dce-51c8-986c-72da0b5ce186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177743} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca62f9b-a692-5595-9a25-fca8bc78466f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177744} {:answer/id #uuid "35fa39a4-59ff-5366-ae36-6f059d5ed2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177745} {:answer/id #uuid "5668bdea-843a-5cfa-8097-eb18d699e389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177746} {:answer/id #uuid "585b6fcc-929d-583b-9c4c-753fa60c238c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "585b6fcc-929d-583b-9c4c-753fa60c238c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177747} :answer-field/id #uuid "67918ca8-107f-5765-a5d1-754dd168bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177742}] :question/id #uuid "76c1a939-3dff-51ed-a845-c6333747802b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125424" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x, y) = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2} + y^{2}}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $∇f$ is conservative\n2. $∮_(C)∇f \\cdot dr = 0$ for any closed path $C ∈ R^{2}$\n3. $∫_(C)∇f \\cdot dr = 0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C ∈ R^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "553c3284-1cc8-5cd6-bf53-43e787406d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177755} {:answer/id #uuid "6492d3b1-fbd2-5113-a984-3816de854e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177756} {:answer/id #uuid "67cdb085-05c1-5c1d-a6f0-ebab6a3d0019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177757} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8ca0af-0e24-5ef6-be62-3bc3b0bcb776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177758} {:answer/id #uuid "71e7bf95-6f9c-59a9-8bf1-fa297751aed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "553c3284-1cc8-5cd6-bf53-43e787406d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177755} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dc50a4-262e-58bd-857d-bd66b3231b7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177754}] :question/id #uuid "81113ee4-2843-5316-a3de-8f3bdbd20c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125485" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x, y) = \\frac{5}{y}i + \\frac{1}{x}j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25x^{2} + y^{2} = 25$ oriented counterclockwise."}]}
